[b]Elliott Wave Radar — rule-validated wave counting that admits when there is no count[/b]

[b]What it does[/b]
Most auto-Elliott tools label every swing, whether the result is a legal wave count or not. Elliott Wave Radar takes the opposite approach: it builds ZigZag pivots, tests every possible interpretation against the hard Elliott Wave rules — wave points are picked from all stored pivots, so a wave may contain smaller swings of its own — and only draws a count when one passes. It shows the raw wave ratios of the count, and it is a display of the current structure — no setup calls, no entry signals. When nothing qualifies, the dashboard says "No valid count" — no forced labels.

[b]Hard rules enforced[/b]
[list]
[*]Wave 2 never retraces beyond the start of Wave 1
[*]Wave 3 is never the shortest of waves 1, 3 and 5
[*]Wave 4 never enters Wave 1 price territory (overlapping structures are left to the diagonal fallback)
[*]Wave 3 must exceed the end of Wave 1, Wave 5 the end of Wave 3
[*]When Wave 3 is shorter than Wave 1, Wave 5 may not exceed Wave 3 — a W1-W4 count draws this limit as "W5 max" and breaks when price runs beyond it
[*]Sub-structure on the finer pivot degree: impulse 5-3-5-3-5, zigzag 5-3-5, flat 3-3-5 — only clear results reject
[*]Diagonals by position: a leading diagonal only as W1 or A, an ending diagonal only as W5 or C, never as W3
[*]Zigzag: B retraces less than 90 % of A; flat: 90 % or more; C travels beyond the A end
[/list]

Fibonacci ratios, the one-extension guideline and the size of swings inside a wave are guidelines: they shape the fit, they never reject a count on their own.

[b]Raw ratios[/b]
The dashboard shows the measured ratios of the active count — W2/W1, W3/W1, W4/W3, W5/W1 for impulses, B/A and C/A for corrections — so you can compare them with the Fibonacci guidelines yourself. Rule-valid interpretations whose ratios sit too far from the usual Fibonacci guidelines are dropped (adjustable minimum fit). If several remain, they are ranked by that fit with a small preference for more complete structures; the top one is shown and the next one appears as the alt count. The fit measures closeness to guideline ratios, not the probability that a count is correct.

[b]Corrections[/b]
[list]
[*]Zigzag A-B-C (B retraces less than 90 % of A, C travels beyond A)
[*]Flat — regular and expanded (B retraces 90 % of A or more)
[*]A-B after impulse: after a completed impulse plus confirmed A-B, C projections at 1.0 / 1.618 × A — with the impulse extreme as monitored invalidation. When A is not a clear five, the same waves also run as alt count "W1-W2 (leading diagonal?)"
[/list]

[b]Partial counts and projections[/b]
[list]
[*]After a first wave and its pullback: labeled "1-2 / A-B", because it can start an impulse or a correction. The next waves decide — a pullback after W3 / C outside W1 territory makes it W1-W4, otherwise a valid zigzag or flat makes it A-B-C. W2/B levels at 38.2 / 50 / 61.8 / 78.6 % and W3/C projections at 1.0 / 1.618 × W1 (W3 also 2.618). Every possible W1 start is tested; the complete count decides
[*]After W1–W4 confirm: W1-W4 count with projections at 0.618 / 1.0 / 1.618 × W1
[*]After a complete impulse: retracement levels at 38.2 / 50 / 61.8 %
[*]Invalidation level drawn and monitored bar-by-bar — if price breaks it, the count is removed and an alert fires
[/list]

[b]Provisional fallbacks[/b]
Only when no ordinary count fits, two rarer patterns are tried and labeled as provisional:
[list]
[*]Diagonal (searched over all stored pivots): W4 overlaps W1, contracting or expanding wedge, sub-structure checked. Leading or ending can only be guessed without the parent wave's position, hence the "?"
[*]W-X-Y (searched over all stored pivots): W, X and Y each a zigzag or flat
[/list]
Triangles and nested combinations are not modeled.

[b]Running count[/b]
An active count is continued at every new pivot — 1-2 / A-B becomes W1-W4 or A-B-C, then the impulse, then A-B and A-B-C — and is replaced only when it breaks a rule or its correction completes. A new local pattern cannot push aside a structure that is still intact. When a 1-2 / A-B can be read as both W1-W4 and A-B-C, both readings run in parallel: the better fit is shown, the other is the running alt count and takes over if the first one breaks. When price runs beyond the W5 end of the active impulse, W5 is treated as extending.

[b]Sub-structure[/b]
A second, finer pivot degree checks each wave: its internal swings are tested for a 5-wave impulse under the same hard rules. The dashboard shows the result per wave — 5 (five waves fit), 3 (exactly three swings, corrective), unclear (more swings without a 5-wave fit), 1 (single leg) or ? (no sub data). Only clear results reject: three swings rule out a motive wave, five waves rule out a B wave or the A of a flat. The check is one level deep.

[b]Last impulse[/b]
The last completed impulse stays on the chart (faded) while the next structure develops.

[b]Dashboard[/b]
Current count and direction, raw ratios, invalidation level, sub-structure per wave, the runner-up interpretation (alt count), and how many interpretations passed the hard rules at the last pivot and how many fell below the Fib fit.

[b]Alerts[/b]
Event alerts: impulse / zigzag / flat count complete · A-B after impulse / 1-2 or A-B / W1-W4 count active · close beyond wave-1 end · count invalidated.

[b]Honest limitations[/b]
Counts use confirmed pivots only (labels appear with the pivot confirmation delay — no repainting, no real-time extreme calls). Only the most recent structure is labeled, one degree is counted, diagonals and W-X-Y are provisional fallbacks, and triangles are not modeled — such phases show "No valid count".

